{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 42","reason":"23,232 = 2^6 * 3 * 11^2, so its number of positive divisors is (6+1)(1+1)(2+1) = 42.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_8_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Choose prime exponents in a positive divisor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (7)","reason":"2020 = 2²·5·101 has (2+1)(1+1)(1+1) = 12 divisors. Exactly 1, 2, 5, 101, and 4 have at most three divisors, leaving 12 − 5 = 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_8_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Use the prime factorization to count divisors and remove the exceptions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2","reason":"Write n=7^e u with 7 not dividing u, and let D be the number of divisors of u. Then (e+1)D=60 and (e+2)D=80. Dividing gives (e+2)/(e+1)=4/3, hence e=2. Therefore the greatest k is 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization"],"id":"separate-the-seven-factor","label":"Separate the power of 7","methodDirectNodeIds":{"separate-the-seven-factor":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 11","reason":"There are (n+1)^2 divisors, and complementary divisors have logarithms summing to n. Hence the total is n(n+1)^2/2 = 792. For n = 11 this is 11 times 144 divided by 2, or 792; the expression is strictly increasing for nonnegative integers.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complementary-divisor-pairs","label":"Pair complementary divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complementary-divisor-pairs":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h043"],"id":"complementary-divisors","label":"Pair complementary divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complementary-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h043"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h043"],"id":"solution:sum-prime-exponents:77844532b9ae","label":"Sum the prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h044","cnm_h204","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 11","reason":"We have τ(d)=9, so d is p⁸ or p²q² for distinct primes. Joey's stated two-digit future age forces d<100, leaving only d=36. Joey's age is then t+37, so his next multiple birthday occurs at t=37, since 37 is prime. Joey will be 74, whose digits sum to 11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Uniqueness uses the question's explicit two-digit condition. The nine-birthday condition alone permits other age differences."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"age-differences-and-divisors","label":"Turn age multiples into divisor counts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"age-differences-and-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 22","reason":"For n>1, the three floors differ only when n divides 999 or 1000. In those cases their sum is respectively 3q-1 or 3q-2, so it is not divisible by 3. The two nontrivial divisor sets are disjoint. Since 999=3^3*37 has 8 divisors and 1000=2^3*5^3 has 16, the count is 7+15=22. The case n=1 does not qualify.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"floor-jumps-and-divisors","label":"Find where the floors increase","methodDirectNodeIds":{"floor-jumps-and-divisors":["math:floor-ceiling","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 1:14","reason":"N=2^3 M with M odd. If S is the sum of the divisors of M, the odd-divisor sum is S and the even-divisor sum is (2+4+8)S=14S. The ratio is 1:14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"group-by-odd-part","label":"Group divisors by odd part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"group-by-odd-part":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1:14","reason":"N = 2³·3⁵·5·7·17². If S is the sum of its odd divisors, the even divisors have sum (2+4+8)S = 14S. The requested ratio is therefore 1:14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"group-by-odd-part","label":"Group divisors by odd part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"group-by-odd-part":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 10","reason":"For n≤50, the possible first iterates are 2,4,6,8,10,12,16,18,20; only 12,18,20 eventually reach 12. Thus τ(n) must be 6,9,10. The eight numbers with six divisors are 12,18,20,28,32,44,45,50; the other two are 36 and 48. The total is 10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Classify divisor counts and their iteration paths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h200"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 1/192","reason":"f(768) = 4(2^9-1)/768 = 511/192, while f(384) = 4(2^8-1)/384 = 510/192. Their difference is 1/192.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cancel-doubled-divisors","label":"Cancel the even divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-doubled-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cancel-reciprocal-divisors","label":"Sum reciprocals of divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-reciprocal-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"compute-divisor-sums","label":"Compute the divisor sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compute-divisor-sums":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h210"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 123","reason":"30^4=2^4*3^4*5^4 has (4+1)^3=125 positive divisors. Removing 1 and 30^4 leaves 123.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"],"id":"count-prime-exponents","label":"Choose each prime exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 7 integers","reason":"Divisors pair as d and n/d, with an unpaired divisor only when n is a square. The positive squares below 50 are 1,4,9,16,25,36,49, giving seven.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p156"],"id":"pair-complementary-divisors","label":"Pair the divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-complementary-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_p153","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 1","reason":"The possible forms are m=p³, with divisor sum 1+p+p²+p³, or m=pq for distinct primes, with sum (p+1)(q+1). No prime cube gives a sum in the interval. The product form leaves only 2010, 2012, and 2016 as candidates. For 2010, p=2 would require composite q=669. For 2012, dividing both even factors by 2 gives a product of 503, forcing a factor 1 and hence a nonprime p=1. Finally, 2016=(3+1)(503+1), with 503 prime. Only 2016 is nice.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_p156"],"id":"four-divisor-classification","label":"Classify numbers with four divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"four-divisor-classification":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1/19","reason":"The exponent of 2 in 21! is 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 18. For every divisor of its odd part, there are 19 divisors obtained using exponents 0 through 18 of 2, and exactly one is odd. The probability is therefore 1/19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"odd-part-divisor-groups","label":"Group divisors by odd part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"odd-part-divisor-groups":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1/19","reason":"The exponent of 2 in 21! is floor(21/2) + floor(21/4) + floor(21/8) + floor(21/16) = 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 18. An odd divisor uses exponent 0 rather than any of the 19 possible exponents of 2; all other exponent choices cancel, giving 1/19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-two-exponent-choices","label":"Count the choices for the power of two","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-two-exponent-choices":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h137"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h137"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 11","reason":"Let N be Chloe's age minus Zoe's age. At Zoe's age t, Chloe's age is a multiple of t exactly when t divides N. Nine divisors force N = p^8 or N = p²r² for distinct primes p,r. The requested two-digit future age of Joey permits only N = 36. Joey's age is then t + 37, so his next qualifying birthday occurs at t = 37, when he is 74. The digit sum is 11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The phrase 'two digits' restricts Joey's requested future age to less than 100. Without that restriction or another age bound, the nine-birthday condition allows other age gaps.","Uniqueness uses the question's explicit two-digit condition. The nine-birthday condition alone permits other age differences."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"birthday-divisors","label":"Turn birthdays into divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"birthday-divisors":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 23","reason":"Since 12!=2^10·3^5·5²·7·11, it has 11·6·3·2·2=792 positive divisors. Square divisors have even exponents, giving 6·3·2·1·1=36 choices. The probability is 36/792=1/22, so m+n=23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponents","label":"Count prime exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponents":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (9; N=2520)","reason":"For n=product p^e, f(n)^3=product (e+1)^3/p^e. Each factor's successive ratio is ((e+2)/(e+1))^3/p, decreasing with e. The unique maximizing exponents are 3 for p=2, 2 for p=3, 1 for p=5 and 7, and 0 for every other prime. Thus N=2^3*3^2*5*7=2520, whose digit sum is 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-exponent-ratios","label":"Optimize each prime exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponent-ratios":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:extremal-principle","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:extremal-principle","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (58)","reason":"Because 2021=43·47, the exponent patterns are (2020) or (46,42). The least integer is 2^46·3^42, which is smaller than 2^2020. It equals 16·6^42, so m+k=16+42=58.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_10B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"classify-prime-exponents","label":"Count divisors using exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:extremal-principle","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:extremal-principle","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 10","reason":"For n≤50, the possible divisor counts are 1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10. Iteration of t↦2d(t) reaches 12 exactly when d(n) is 6, 9, or 10; all other cases reach 8. These give respectively 8, 1, and 1 integers: 12,18,20,28,32,36,44,45,48,50.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-pattern-iteration","label":"Follow divisor counts by prime pattern","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-pattern-iteration":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:function-composition-iteration","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:function-composition-iteration","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 4319","reason":"The remainders must be 0,1,…,z-1, so 2025≡1 mod z. Conversely, this congruence makes every remainder equal to its input x. Thus S consists of the divisors greater than 1 of 2024=2³·11·23. Their sum is (1+2+4+8)(1+11)(1+23)-1=4319.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"ordered-full-remainder-list","label":"Order the full remainder list","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ordered-full-remainder-list":["math:modular-congruence","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"first-wraparound","label":"Find the first wraparound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"first-wraparound":["math:modular-congruence","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 35","reason":"Write n=2^a3^b m with gcd(m,6)=1 and t=d(m). Then (a+2)(b+1)t=28 and (a+1)(b+2)t=30. Since t divides 2, checking t=1,2 gives only (a,b,t)=(5,3,1). Thus d(6n)=7·5=35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1996_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153"],"id":"track-prime-exponents","label":"Track the exponents of 2 and 3","methodDirectNodeIds":{"track-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5 denominators","reason":"Let k=10a+b. The reduced denominator is 99/gcd(k,99). Since 99=3^2·11 and k is neither 0 nor 99, the possible denominators are precisely 3, 9, 11, 33, and 99. Each is attained by taking k=99/d.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p120","cnm_040","cnm_p158","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"decimal-shift-and-divisors","label":"Convert the decimal and classify its denominators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"decimal-shift-and-divisors":["math:repeating-decimal-fractions","cnm_p168","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:repeating-decimal-fractions","cnm_p168","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_p120","cnm_040","cnm_p158","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 37","reason":"Since 201^9=3^9·67^9, a divisor is 3^a·67^b with 0≤a,b≤9. Squares give 5²=25 exponent choices, cubes give 4²=16, and sixth powers give 2²=4. The requested union has 25+16−4=37 divisors.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-exponents-and-overlap","label":"Count exponent choices and subtract overlap","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponents-and-overlap":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231","cnm_h009"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231","cnm_h009"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 216","reason":"N = (69+1)^5 = 70^5 = 2^5·5^5·7^5. Its number of positive divisors is (5+1)^3 = 216.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1986_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143","cnm_h231"],"id":"binomial-prime-exponents","label":"Recognize a fifth power and count exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"binomial-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h240","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h240","math:prime-factorization","cnm_143","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 202","reason":"If x = ∏pᵢ^(3eᵢ), then d = ∏(3eᵢ + 1), so d ≡ 1 modulo 3. Only 202 has that residue among the choices, and x = 2²⁰¹ = (2⁶⁷)³ has exactly 202 positive divisors.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"prime-exponents-and-remainders","label":"Count divisors using prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponents-and-remainders":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 672","reason":"The product has factorization 2^30·3^13·5^5·7^3. A square divisor has 16, 7, 3, and 2 choices for those prime exponents, respectively, giving 16·7·3·2=672.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation","cnm_h231"],"id":"count-even-prime-exponents","label":"Count the even prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-even-prime-exponents":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 6","reason":"The product is 2⁸3⁴5²7. A cube divisor has three choices for its exponent of 2, two for its exponent of 3, and only zero for the other exponents. Thus there are 3 × 2 = 6 positive cube divisors.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","cnm_143","cnm_h231"],"id":"cube-divisor-exponents","label":"Choose the prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-divisor-exponents":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:factorial-notation","cnm_143","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 13","reason":"The condition is b≥4 and b divides 2013-3=2010. Since 2010=2·3·5·67, it has 2^4=16 positive divisors. Excluding 1, 2, and 3 leaves 13 valid bases.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"],"id":"place-value-and-divisors","label":"Translate the last digit into a divisor condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"place-value-and-divisors":["cnm_024","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_p050","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1080π","reason":"Using cos(4x)=2cos^2(2x)-1 reduces the equation to cos(2x)cos(2014π^2/x)=1. Thus 2x=aπ and 2014π^2/x=bπ for positive integers a,b of the same parity. Since ab=4028=4·19·53, both are even. Writing a=2d and b=2e gives de=1007 and x=dπ. The solutions are π,19π,53π,1007π, whose sum is 1080π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h073","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"cosine-extremes-and-divisors","label":"Cosine extreme values and factor pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-extremes-and-divisors":["cnm_h068","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h068","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h073","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 325","reason":"Let the exponents of 2, 5, and 11 in n be a, b, and c. Their contributions to the given divisor count are 3a+2, 3b+2, and 3c+2, each greater than 1. Since 110 = 2·5·11 is squarefree, these factors must be 2, 5, and 11 in some order, with no other primes in n. Thus {a,b,c} = {0,1,3}, and 3 does not divide n. Therefore 81n⁴ has 5·1·5·13 = 325 positive divisors.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_143","cnm_h231"],"id":"divisor-count-factor-constraints","label":"Constrain the prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"divisor-count-factor-constraints":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_143","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 325","reason":"The factors 3e+2 for primes 2, 5, and 11 must be 2, 5, and 11 in some order, since their product already requires all three prime factors of 110. Thus n has exponents 0, 1, and 3 on those primes and no others. Consequently d(81n⁴)=5·1·5·13=325.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_h231","cnm_143"],"id":"force-prime-exponents","label":"Use the prime exponents in the divisor count","methodDirectNodeIds":{"force-prime-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_h231","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 48","reason":"Since 5!=2³·3·5 and 10!=2⁸·3⁴·5²·7, the equality forces n=2^u·3^v·5³·7^w, where 3≤u≤8, 1≤v≤4, and w is 0 or 1. No other primes can divide n. There are 6·4·2=48 choices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-divisor-cofactors","label":"Turn the equation into a divisor count","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-divisor-cofactors":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 9/200","reason":"The contributing numbers are 11k for 1≤k≤9. Since 11 does not divide k, their divisors split equally into d and 11d for d dividing k. Therefore the probability is (9/100)(1/2)=9/200.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-divisors-by-exponents","label":"Count divisors using prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-divisors-by-exponents":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponent-choices","label":"Count exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponent-choices":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h245","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h245","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 6","reason":"The equation gives x=3+12/k, which is integral exactly when positive k divides 12. Its positive divisors are 1,2,3,4,6,12, giving six choices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponent-choices","label":"Count prime-exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponent-choices":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 9","reason":"Since MN=36=2²·3², there are (2+1)(2+1)=9 choices for M. Each has the unique partner N=36/M, giving 9 ordered pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10B_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponent-choices","label":"Count prime-exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponent-choices":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 7","reason":"Set d=30-n. The quotient is a positive integer exactly when d is a positive divisor of 30 smaller than 30. These divisors are 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, and 15, giving seven values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponent-choices","label":"Count divisors by prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponent-choices":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (3, since T = 12 minutes)","reason":"Any return time is an integer. Among times 1 through 11, at most four indices from 1 through 10 divide the time, with four occurring at 6, 8, and 10. At time 12, horses 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 are all at the start. Thus T = 12 and its digit sum is 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-divisor-count","label":"Use prime exponents to bound divisor counts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-divisor-count":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 6","reason":"The divisors are 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 21, and 42. Pairing complementary divisors gives N = 42^4, whose units digit is the units digit of 2·2·2·2 = 16, namely 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pair-complementary-divisors","label":"Pair the divisors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-complementary-divisors":["cnm_p153","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p153","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 7","reason":"Because 36=2^2×3^2, it has (2+1)(2+1)=9 positive divisors. Excluding 1 and 36 leaves 7 possibilities, yielding coat counts 1,2,3,5,8,11,17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-exponent-divisor-count","label":"Count divisors by prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponent-divisor-count":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 60","reason":"Both 6 and 15 divide the total, so it is a multiple of 30. The number 30 has only 8 divisors, while 60=2²×3×5 has (2+1)(1+1)(1+1)=12 divisors. Since 30 is the only smaller positive multiple of 30, the minimum is 60.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-exponent-divisor-count","label":"Count divisors from prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponent-divisor-count":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (7)","reason":"The sum formula gives k(2a+k-1)=690. Since a≥1, 2≤k≤25. The admissible lengths are 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 23; each gives a positive integer a, yielding seven representations.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"odd-divisor-correspondence","label":"Count through odd divisors","nodeIds":["math:bijective-counting","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h204","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_h231"],"id":"solution:odd-divisor-correspondence:19726c1673f3","label":"Pair factors instead of listing lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"odd-divisor-correspondence":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:bijective-counting","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:bijective-counting","math:prime-factorization","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h204","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 784","reason":"Write B = (b,b) and D = (d,kd), with b,d positive integers. The area is bd(k - 1), so b·d·(k - 1) = 10^6 = 2^6·5^6. For each prime, its exponent 6 can be distributed among three factors in C(8,2) = 28 ways. These independent choices give 28^2 = 784 distinct parallelograms.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-divisor-counts","label":"Sum the remaining-factor divisor counts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-divisor-counts":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h204","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h204","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 5","reason":"If the first term is a, then 153=n(a+n-1). Thus n must divide 153=3^2·17. Its divisors greater than 1 are 3, 9, 17, 51, and 153, each giving an integer a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_p153","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"arithmetic-sum-divisors","label":"Turn the sum condition into divisibility","methodDirectNodeIds":{"arithmetic-sum-divisors":["cnm_h204","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h204","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h202","cnm_p153","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 22","reason":"An interior angle is 180-360/n, integral exactly when n divides 360. Since 360=2³·3²·5, it has 4·3·2=24 positive divisors. Excluding 1 and 2 leaves 22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"],"id":"prime-exponent-count","label":"Count divisors using prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-exponent-count":["cnm_181","cnm_p153","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_181","cnm_p153","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_180","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (17)","reason":"Tangency gives r=(a+b-c)/2 or r=(a+b+c)/2. The first case corresponds to positive ordered factors uv=2r², with a=2r+u and b=2r+v. The second corresponds to factors satisfying r<u,v<2r. The total segment counts for r=1,...,6 are 2,4,6,6,6,14. At r=6, the smallest and largest hypotenuses are 5 from (3,4,5) and 85 from (13,84,85). Thus c14/c1=85/5=17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"distance-to-intercept-line","label":"Apply the line-circle distance condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distance-to-intercept-line":["cnm_h170","cnm_h174","cnm_h179","cnm_174","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h170","cnm_h174","cnm_h179","cnm_174","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p188"],"id":"equal-tangent-segments","label":"Use equal tangent lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangent-segments":["cnm_263","cnm_128","cnm_174","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_p188","cnm_128","cnm_174","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:divisor-count-and-sum","status":"mapped","total":46}
